  m_text: 'Balloon

    The Archimedean force is the upward thrust that a fluid (water,
    air) exerts on an immersed object. Such a body in a fluid receives an upward
    thrust equal to the weight of the displaced fluid.

    Example, if you immerse
    a ball in water, the water that "makes room" for the ball weighs a certain amount,
    that is exactly the push you feel.


    Knowing that the force is equal to:                               
    the Archimedes Force will be equal to



    A hot air balloon, even if initially
    anchored, if it is released will be subject by the Archimedes Force. In fact,
    in the force balance equation:



    In this case, we want to be able to exploit
    the equation of the forces applied on the body in the case:




    The only
    way is to increase the weight in the balloon basket. Finally, assuming that the
    volume V is practically the volume of air displaced by the balloon, applying
    the formula:



    We should arrive at the solution by obtaining the density
    of the balloon.








'
